WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.820
&gt;&gt; Thanks for coming
and thanks Sumit

00:00:06.821 --> 00:00:10.990
for offering your
precious time to visit us.

00:00:10.991 --> 00:00:13.165
Actually, he is one of us.

00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:16.200
He started Microsoft
Research, and of course,

00:00:16.201 --> 00:00:19.775
we were very proud of
what he has achieved.

00:00:19.776 --> 00:00:22.080
He started in rise.

00:00:22.081 --> 00:00:25.840
He's the main architect
of Pendleton and leads,

00:00:25.841 --> 00:00:27.530
which is a data wrangling tool,

00:00:27.531 --> 00:00:28.990
a data ingestion tool.

00:00:28.991 --> 00:00:32.435
He leads the PROSE group
that does that.

00:00:32.436 --> 00:00:34.420
When I read the abstract,

00:00:34.421 --> 00:00:36.150
he was joking about

00:00:36.151 --> 00:00:40.030
a mid-life crisis and it's
shocking. He's way too young.

00:00:40.031 --> 00:00:43.055
Rico and I, we can joke
about our mid-life crisis,

00:00:43.056 --> 00:00:45.840
but you haven't
earned the right yet.

00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:47.815
You're way too young for that.

00:00:47.816 --> 00:00:52.310
But anyway we're really
looking forward to your talk.

00:00:52.311 --> 00:00:56.290
&gt;&gt; Okay, thanks Donald
for the introduction.

00:00:56.291 --> 00:00:58.900
It's an awesome feeling

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:01.085
to be back in
my home building here.

00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:05.100
So my research career
has not spend 18 years.

00:01:05.101 --> 00:01:08.575
And in this second half
of this career,

00:01:08.576 --> 00:01:10.525
I have led a different

00:01:10.526 --> 00:01:12.630
research lifestyle
than what I had in

00:01:12.631 --> 00:01:14.570
the first half
and this has given

00:01:14.571 --> 00:01:17.495
me much more personal
satisfaction.

00:01:17.496 --> 00:01:21.190
And this is a story that I
want to share with you today.

00:01:21.191 --> 00:01:26.240
Unsurprisingly, the story
starts with my mid-life crisis.

00:01:26.241 --> 00:01:31.020
So I saved enough to buy my BMW.

00:01:31.021 --> 00:01:34.395
I had won some amazing awards.

00:01:34.396 --> 00:01:37.010
I had a decent amount
of publications,

00:01:37.011 --> 00:01:41.510
citation counts, H-index,
and it was climbing steadily.

00:01:41.511 --> 00:01:43.985
But then there wasn't-.

00:01:43.986 --> 00:01:49.880
&gt;&gt; How much for BMW?

00:01:49.881 --> 00:01:57.165
&gt;&gt; But some form of
deeper meaning was lacking.

00:01:57.166 --> 00:02:00.210
The work that I was
doing, I felt was not

00:02:00.211 --> 00:02:03.210
as much significant for

00:02:03.211 --> 00:02:08.560
the society and the legacy
that it would possibly lead.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:09.800
This inspired me to start

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:12.620
looking for fixing
this situation.

00:02:12.621 --> 00:02:14.570
The idea was clear,

00:02:14.571 --> 00:02:18.755
I wanted to transition to
different value systems.

00:02:18.756 --> 00:02:21.710
Now, by no means,
these value systems are novel.

00:02:21.711 --> 00:02:24.230
In fact, they are very simple,

00:02:24.231 --> 00:02:26.960
but the reason why I am excited
to talk about them today

00:02:26.961 --> 00:02:30.570
is that they are
unreasonably effective.

00:02:30.571 --> 00:02:33.530
In fact, many of
you might actually

00:02:33.531 --> 00:02:36.255
already be following
these kinds of value systems.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.000
However, I have to admit that

00:02:38.001 --> 00:02:41.465
the academic world of which
we are all products off,

00:02:41.466 --> 00:02:43.640
does not emphasize
these value systems

00:02:43.641 --> 00:02:46.280
as much as it should.

00:02:46.281 --> 00:02:51.360
The reason why I called them
Big Bets is because that

00:02:51.361 --> 00:02:53.645
the transition to
these value systems was not

00:02:53.646 --> 00:02:56.655
easy because change
is usually hard.

00:02:56.656 --> 00:03:00.340
I was very intentional
about making the switch.

00:03:00.341 --> 00:03:05.410
This is something that required
a huge amount of effort.

00:03:05.411 --> 00:03:09.125
It makes up for an interesting
story to be shared.

00:03:09.126 --> 00:03:13.085
However, the most
important thing here is

00:03:13.086 --> 00:03:17.525
that these were
just not my bets.

00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:20.710
I did not simply bet
on these value systems,

00:03:20.711 --> 00:03:22.625
but I also bet on the people

00:03:22.626 --> 00:03:24.780
who are involved
with this transition.

00:03:24.781 --> 00:03:28.795
This was a bet that
even those people made.

00:03:28.796 --> 00:03:31.220
This was a bet that
was even made by

00:03:31.221 --> 00:03:34.275
the people who funded
these arrangements.

00:03:34.276 --> 00:03:39.250
So this is a story of
several of us together.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.280
Before I describe
these four bets,

00:03:45.281 --> 00:03:47.560
let me actually
provide some context in

00:03:47.561 --> 00:03:49.970
terms of the research agenda
that I was working on,

00:03:49.971 --> 00:03:53.865
and I would explain these bets
in that specific context.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:57.210
The problem that
had taken most of

00:03:57.211 --> 00:04:00.580
my interest was
the soft program synthesis.

00:04:00.581 --> 00:04:04.930
The goal here is to automatically
search for a program in

00:04:04.931 --> 00:04:07.425
an underlying
domain-specific language

00:04:07.426 --> 00:04:11.290
from some natural form of
intent provided by the user,

00:04:11.291 --> 00:04:12.890
either in the form
of input/output

00:04:12.891 --> 00:04:15.265
examples or natural language.

00:04:15.266 --> 00:04:17.780
In fact, this had
been an old problem,

00:04:17.781 --> 00:04:19.825
almost as old programming,

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:23.270
but not much success
was achieved back then.

00:04:23.271 --> 00:04:25.120
In the past decade, we have seen

00:04:25.121 --> 00:04:27.310
some significant advances
in this area.

00:04:27.311 --> 00:04:32.505
Thanks to much better algorithms
and faster machines.

00:04:32.506 --> 00:04:37.190
In fact, just to boast
what we have achieved now,

00:04:37.191 --> 00:04:38.560
a few months ago,

00:04:38.561 --> 00:04:42.195
we conducted an experiment
with some Python programmers.

00:04:42.196 --> 00:04:45.185
We gave them
a data cleaning task,

00:04:45.186 --> 00:04:46.750
and they took on average,

00:04:46.751 --> 00:04:49.060
30 minutes to finish this task.

00:04:49.061 --> 00:04:51.160
We can do the same task using

00:04:51.161 --> 00:04:55.745
our program synthesis APIs
in 30 seconds.

00:04:55.746 --> 00:05:00.850
Program synthesis can really
improve the productivity of

00:05:00.851 --> 00:05:02.770
developers by
one to two hours of

00:05:02.771 --> 00:05:05.630
magnitude in
certain tasks domains.

00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:08.620
But, what gets me
even more excited

00:05:08.621 --> 00:05:11.600
is the potential that it

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:13.930
can enable non-programmers to

00:05:13.931 --> 00:05:17.270
create small scripts for
automating repetitive tasks.

00:05:17.271 --> 00:05:19.350
Ninety nine percent
people who have

00:05:19.351 --> 00:05:21.605
computers do not
know programming.

00:05:21.606 --> 00:05:24.090
And these people struggle
with repetitive tasks in

00:05:24.091 --> 00:05:27.825
their lives but these
are not stupid people.

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:30.420
They just don't know the
artificial programming language

00:05:30.421 --> 00:05:32.180
of speaking to a computer.

00:05:32.181 --> 00:05:35.320
But program synthesis
technologies

00:05:35.321 --> 00:05:36.370
can enable these people to

00:05:36.371 --> 00:05:38.405
achieve much more
in their lives now,

00:05:38.406 --> 00:05:41.685
because they can use computers
much more effectively.

00:05:41.686 --> 00:05:46.370
This is a context of
the research that I was in,

00:05:46.371 --> 00:05:48.960
and when I had
this mid-life crisis,

00:05:48.961 --> 00:05:52.575
I was working on
program synthesis for

00:05:52.576 --> 00:05:54.670
trying to help
algorithm designers

00:05:54.671 --> 00:05:56.820
and hardcore
software developers.

00:05:56.821 --> 00:06:00.170
And the kinds of tasks that
we were looking at was to

00:06:00.171 --> 00:06:04.740
synthesize textbook algorithms
like sorting algorithms,

00:06:04.741 --> 00:06:07.455
or to synthesize
tricky pieces of

00:06:07.456 --> 00:06:09.120
code that even humans would

00:06:09.121 --> 00:06:12.970
take years or two
to come up with.

00:06:12.971 --> 00:06:15.685
And this had to change.

00:06:15.686 --> 00:06:17.485
So the first bet that I

00:06:17.486 --> 00:06:20.965
placed was to be able to
connect to the customers

00:06:20.966 --> 00:06:22.520
more directly
because this kind of

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:24.850
program synthesis was not

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:26.600
able to achieve
the kind of dream

00:06:26.601 --> 00:06:30.255
that I just outlined
earlier to be able to help

00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:32.520
developers in their regular
tasks or to be able to

00:06:32.521 --> 00:06:33.570
help non-programmers with

00:06:33.571 --> 00:06:35.795
the task that they
struggle with.

00:06:35.796 --> 00:06:38.250
In this old world,

00:06:38.251 --> 00:06:40.540
the way I would choose
the problem that I

00:06:40.541 --> 00:06:42.760
would go solve would

00:06:42.761 --> 00:06:45.545
be mostly inspired by

00:06:45.546 --> 00:06:46.930
what will help me publish

00:06:46.931 --> 00:06:48.770
a paper in a
top-tier conference.

00:06:48.771 --> 00:06:50.210
What is the next incremental

00:06:50.211 --> 00:06:51.760
or foundational
improvement that I can

00:06:51.761 --> 00:06:52.890
do which can get me

00:06:52.891 --> 00:06:55.290
a next paper in a top-tier
research conference.

00:06:55.291 --> 00:06:57.390
And the way I would define

00:06:57.391 --> 00:06:59.570
my success metrics would be

00:06:59.571 --> 00:07:03.055
to either come up with
a benchmark myself.

00:07:03.056 --> 00:07:05.420
In fact, I have written
papers which just get

00:07:05.421 --> 00:07:07.170
cited by the benchmarks
that I chose

00:07:07.171 --> 00:07:08.640
there and those benchmarks
are just made

00:07:08.641 --> 00:07:13.370
up from some imagination
one afternoon.

00:07:13.371 --> 00:07:15.300
The other way I would choose the

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:16.470
benchmarks would be to just

00:07:16.471 --> 00:07:19.625
follow what other people
would use in their papers.

00:07:19.626 --> 00:07:21.380
And this is the stuff that had

00:07:21.381 --> 00:07:22.590
to change if I really wanted to

00:07:22.591 --> 00:07:26.525
connect with real people
and change their lives.

00:07:26.526 --> 00:07:31.510
So in this new world, I found
a lot of inspiration from

00:07:31.511 --> 00:07:34.300
help forums by trying to

00:07:34.301 --> 00:07:39.150
understand what were the pain
points of different users.

00:07:39.151 --> 00:07:41.070
In fact, more
recently, we have been

00:07:41.071 --> 00:07:43.235
conducting exercises
like Moneyball.

00:07:43.236 --> 00:07:44.910
Wherefore, six weeks we do

00:07:44.911 --> 00:07:47.640
nothing but just try to
connect to customers,

00:07:47.641 --> 00:07:50.470
we write scripts to figure
out contact information of

00:07:50.471 --> 00:07:54.325
customers from different
social networking sites.

00:07:54.326 --> 00:07:58.135
We email them to try
to set up a phone call.

00:07:58.136 --> 00:07:59.960
If phone call looks promising,

00:07:59.961 --> 00:08:02.465
we invite them to
a workshop at Microsoft.

00:08:02.466 --> 00:08:03.970
After running that workshop,

00:08:03.971 --> 00:08:05.550
we figure out what did
not work well and we

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:08.085
repeat the process for
the other three weeks.

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:10.140
In fact, when we
do our releases,

00:08:10.141 --> 00:08:11.650
we are very cognizant
of putting in

00:08:11.651 --> 00:08:16.620
proper telemetry and be able
to get feedback from users.

00:08:16.621 --> 00:08:20.800
So this is a world
that I wanted to be

00:08:20.801 --> 00:08:24.795
in and the transition
point came.

00:08:24.796 --> 00:08:27.590
So, there is this one instant
that I like to share here.

00:08:27.591 --> 00:08:30.960
So I was coming back
in an airplane after

00:08:30.961 --> 00:08:34.540
presenting some program
synthesis innovations at

00:08:34.541 --> 00:08:36.160
the workshop and
there was a lady

00:08:36.161 --> 00:08:37.980
sitting next to me
and she was very

00:08:37.981 --> 00:08:39.060
impressed to know that I worked

00:08:39.061 --> 00:08:41.920
for Microsoft and
that I had a PhD.

00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:44.635
So she thought I better
ought to be able to help her

00:08:44.636 --> 00:08:48.015
with the Excel tasks that
she was struggling with.

00:08:48.016 --> 00:08:50.310
She opened up
her machine and asked

00:08:50.311 --> 00:08:53.460
me how she can do one
transformation on her column.

00:08:53.461 --> 00:08:55.800
And at that time, I have
to excuse myself out

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:57.470
of the situation because I

00:08:57.471 --> 00:08:58.720
did not had any knowledge

00:08:58.721 --> 00:09:01.600
about the programming
model inside Excel.

00:09:01.601 --> 00:09:04.510
But that gave me a hint
that these are the kinds of

00:09:04.511 --> 00:09:05.890
simple tasks that I need to

00:09:05.891 --> 00:09:07.760
or I should consider
going after,

00:09:07.761 --> 00:09:09.700
as opposed to trying to come up

00:09:09.701 --> 00:09:11.460
with tricky big
vector algorithms or

00:09:11.461 --> 00:09:13.360
reinvent sorting algorithms
which have been known for

00:09:13.361 --> 00:09:16.550
ages because that research
is not going to help anyone.

00:09:16.551 --> 00:09:20.180
So I spent months
trying to study

00:09:20.181 --> 00:09:22.020
spreadsheet help forums to

00:09:22.021 --> 00:09:25.120
figure out what problem
should I be solving,

00:09:25.121 --> 00:09:30.250
which simplest problem can
yield the biggest benefit,

00:09:30.251 --> 00:09:32.890
and what should be
the initial target benchmarks

00:09:32.891 --> 00:09:35.510
that I should be working with.

00:09:35.511 --> 00:09:37.710
This was a typical interaction

00:09:37.711 --> 00:09:39.630
that I noticed on
the help forum.

00:09:39.631 --> 00:09:43.710
An end user would ask
an expert on the help forum,

00:09:43.711 --> 00:09:45.760
"Can you please
give me a script or

00:09:45.761 --> 00:09:47.385
a program that can convert

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:49.230
the string on the left side of

00:09:49.231 --> 00:09:51.230
this arrow to
this thing on the right

00:09:51.231 --> 00:09:53.060
side of this arrow?".

00:09:53.061 --> 00:09:54.150
The expert would look at this

00:09:54.151 --> 00:09:56.870
input/output example and will

00:09:56.871 --> 00:09:59.680
provide a script that

00:09:59.681 --> 00:10:01.140
would say extract two characters

00:10:01.141 --> 00:10:03.025
starting at the fifth character.

00:10:03.026 --> 00:10:06.080
Now, the end user will take
this program and figure out

00:10:06.081 --> 00:10:09.895
that it doesn't do the right job
at this other input.

00:10:09.896 --> 00:10:13.990
The expert looks at it
again and probably tries

00:10:13.991 --> 00:10:17.450
to have a better model of
the intent that the user has.

00:10:17.451 --> 00:10:22.440
So what do you think is
the end user trying to do here?

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.250
Yes, so extract everything that

00:10:28.251 --> 00:10:30.275
is between the first
two underscores.

00:10:30.276 --> 00:10:32.020
And do you know
how do you program,

00:10:32.021 --> 00:10:34.865
how would you program
this in Excel?

00:10:34.866 --> 00:10:38.485
So Excel has
a programming model,

00:10:38.486 --> 00:10:39.880
and this is one of the simplest

00:10:39.881 --> 00:10:41.770
programs that you can write.

00:10:41.771 --> 00:10:43.960
So even if you know programming,

00:10:43.961 --> 00:10:45.960
you will have to go to
stack overflow or some kind of

00:10:45.961 --> 00:10:47.430
documentation to
figure out what is

00:10:47.431 --> 00:10:49.345
the API that Excel exposes.

00:10:49.346 --> 00:10:50.970
But you are the lucky one,

00:10:50.971 --> 00:10:52.675
the one percent people
who know programming.

00:10:52.676 --> 00:10:54.525
Think about 99 percent people

00:10:54.526 --> 00:10:57.425
who do not know programming,
they will be stuck.

00:10:57.426 --> 00:11:00.935
On this case the end user
takes as a script and run set,

00:11:00.936 --> 00:11:03.250
and sends a thank
you to the expert.

00:11:03.251 --> 00:11:04.850
And this is a feature that we

00:11:04.851 --> 00:11:07.205
designed, which
shipped in Excel,

00:11:07.206 --> 00:11:13.385
in 2013 and was quite it
received quite a good reception.

00:11:13.386 --> 00:11:17.275
So it also led me publishing
this particular paper.

00:11:17.276 --> 00:11:19.470
So this is one of
the necklaced papers

00:11:19.471 --> 00:11:21.060
that I've published
in my career.

00:11:21.061 --> 00:11:22.570
Most of those papers had the

00:11:22.571 --> 00:11:24.410
similar kind of
intellectual depth,

00:11:24.411 --> 00:11:26.970
but this is the one
that I'm most known for.

00:11:26.971 --> 00:11:29.415
So why do you think
this is the case?

00:11:29.416 --> 00:11:31.350
This is because, not only

00:11:31.351 --> 00:11:33.690
this work appeared in
a top-tier conference,

00:11:33.691 --> 00:11:36.100
but this work also solves

00:11:36.101 --> 00:11:40.415
a problem that
real people care about,

00:11:40.416 --> 00:11:43.300
that shipped in Excel,

00:11:43.301 --> 00:11:45.360
and this is something
that I would

00:11:45.361 --> 00:11:47.405
say does not happen that often.

00:11:47.406 --> 00:11:49.060
It is very easy to
do one of the two.

00:11:49.061 --> 00:11:51.130
Write software that is used

00:11:51.131 --> 00:11:54.065
by millions of people
everyone in office does that,

00:11:54.066 --> 00:11:56.955
or write a research paper
and all researchers do that.

00:11:56.956 --> 00:11:59.330
But the intersection
is one which is really

00:11:59.331 --> 00:12:02.905
makes this paper special.

00:12:02.906 --> 00:12:06.810
Now several people
often comment that Sumit

00:12:06.811 --> 00:12:10.410
got lucky because he
worked at Microsoft,

00:12:10.411 --> 00:12:12.885
so he figured out
the problem from there

00:12:12.886 --> 00:12:15.560
and the Excel team you know
gave him the benchmarks.

00:12:15.561 --> 00:12:18.280
The truth is
even the Excel team,

00:12:18.281 --> 00:12:20.355
was inspired from us,

00:12:20.356 --> 00:12:21.960
looking at help forums
to figure out

00:12:21.961 --> 00:12:23.520
what the target
benchmark should be.

00:12:23.521 --> 00:12:25.370
They didn't really give
us that much benchmark,

00:12:25.371 --> 00:12:28.295
they didn't really help us
much in terms of benchmarks.

00:12:28.296 --> 00:12:30.235
And the way I went about

00:12:30.236 --> 00:12:32.555
identifying
the problem definition

00:12:32.556 --> 00:12:34.810
and figuring out the benchmark
is something that

00:12:34.811 --> 00:12:37.260
any academic would have possibly
done themselves as well,

00:12:37.261 --> 00:12:39.205
they didn't need to be
inside Microsoft to do that.

00:12:39.206 --> 00:12:40.975
The question really is,

00:12:40.976 --> 00:12:43.160
would they be willing
to spend three months

00:12:43.161 --> 00:12:46.065
doing nothing just
looking at help forums?

00:12:46.066 --> 00:12:48.840
And that is what
this change was all about.

00:12:48.841 --> 00:12:50.770
That is that the risk,
the bet that I took in

00:12:50.771 --> 00:12:53.590
my career which I think
paid off quite well.

00:12:53.591 --> 00:12:55.360
It really is
a funny story about how

00:12:55.361 --> 00:12:58.020
we even got this paper accepted.

00:12:58.021 --> 00:13:00.830
So few days before
I was about to

00:13:00.831 --> 00:13:03.215
submit this paper
to this conference,

00:13:03.216 --> 00:13:06.210
I started going through
the email where I had

00:13:06.211 --> 00:13:07.960
accepted the invitation to

00:13:07.961 --> 00:13:09.330
be on the PC for
this conference,

00:13:09.331 --> 00:13:10.410
to just see how strict

00:13:10.411 --> 00:13:12.995
the standards would
be for PC submissions.

00:13:12.996 --> 00:13:15.445
And then I realized it said,

00:13:15.446 --> 00:13:19.920
single-authored submissions
from PC members not allowed.

00:13:19.921 --> 00:13:22.950
And then I sent a mail
to the chair saying,

00:13:22.951 --> 00:13:24.970
I believe this is
the most important paper

00:13:24.971 --> 00:13:27.300
in my life that I have
written about until now.

00:13:27.301 --> 00:13:29.440
And I happen to have agreed to

00:13:29.441 --> 00:13:31.295
this weird requirement
that you have.

00:13:31.296 --> 00:13:34.300
Do you want me to include
my wife's name on the paper?

00:13:34.301 --> 00:13:37.790
And then within
a few hours an email

00:13:37.791 --> 00:13:40.820
gets sent out to
all the PC members saying,

00:13:40.821 --> 00:13:42.370
I want to leave this requirement

00:13:42.371 --> 00:13:44.365
unless you know someone
has an objection.

00:13:44.366 --> 00:13:47.110
And that paved the way for
me to submit this paper.

00:13:47.111 --> 00:13:49.620
And this really changed how I

00:13:49.621 --> 00:13:54.075
started conducting this search
after this point onwards.

00:13:54.076 --> 00:13:58.490
After we got addicted
to this kind of success,

00:13:58.491 --> 00:13:59.850
we wanted to now apply

00:13:59.851 --> 00:14:01.780
this kind of
programming by examples,

00:14:01.781 --> 00:14:03.770
to many new different domains.

00:14:03.771 --> 00:14:06.630
So instead of just doing
string transformations,

00:14:06.631 --> 00:14:08.080
we applied it to doing

00:14:08.081 --> 00:14:09.675
number transformations
for instance.

00:14:09.676 --> 00:14:11.200
Even a task as simple as

00:14:11.201 --> 00:14:13.175
rounding a number to
two decimal places,

00:14:13.176 --> 00:14:15.050
requires you to remember
different kinds of

00:14:15.051 --> 00:14:17.055
"Format Descriptors"
in different languages.

00:14:17.056 --> 00:14:18.860
But again examples
had a very natural way

00:14:18.861 --> 00:14:20.390
to specify intent here.

00:14:20.391 --> 00:14:23.410
Or maybe think of a task
which is a bit more involved,

00:14:23.411 --> 00:14:25.750
and you will have to spend
several minutes trying to

00:14:25.751 --> 00:14:28.625
think of a script to
do this conversion.

00:14:28.626 --> 00:14:30.420
But again, these are
the kinds of tasks that

00:14:30.421 --> 00:14:33.050
now we can do with
a few examples.

00:14:33.051 --> 00:14:35.695
We applied it to the domain of

00:14:35.696 --> 00:14:39.615
extracting tabular data
from locked files.

00:14:39.616 --> 00:14:42.865
This is an assignment taken
from a data science class,

00:14:42.866 --> 00:14:45.250
where the instructor
asks the students

00:14:45.251 --> 00:14:47.670
to extract that tabular data

00:14:47.671 --> 00:14:49.130
that you see on the right side,

00:14:49.131 --> 00:14:51.475
and the instructor gives
them a script to start with.

00:14:51.476 --> 00:14:52.955
So the goal is to
take this script

00:14:52.956 --> 00:14:55.645
and build on it to go
from left to right.

00:14:55.646 --> 00:14:57.230
But the expedients
were designed,

00:14:57.231 --> 00:14:58.430
all that the user it has to do,

00:14:58.431 --> 00:15:00.090
is to give an example of two of

00:15:00.091 --> 00:15:02.370
the various fields that
they want to extract,

00:15:02.371 --> 00:15:03.900
and then will
automatically be able to

00:15:03.901 --> 00:15:07.065
extract other instances
of that field.

00:15:07.066 --> 00:15:08.920
We applied it to manipulating

00:15:08.921 --> 00:15:11.785
semi-structured spreadsheets.

00:15:11.786 --> 00:15:13.780
Turns out 50% Excel spreadsheets

00:15:13.781 --> 00:15:15.090
are semi-structured and the task

00:15:15.091 --> 00:15:16.370
of going from the left to

00:15:16.371 --> 00:15:18.485
the right is usually
quite involved.

00:15:18.486 --> 00:15:20.310
But again this
can be facilitated

00:15:20.311 --> 00:15:24.695
by simply a few examples.

00:15:24.696 --> 00:15:28.765
So this application domain
that I mentioned to

00:15:28.766 --> 00:15:30.995
you can be clumped
into what is called,

00:15:30.996 --> 00:15:33.115
data transformations
or data-wrangling.

00:15:33.116 --> 00:15:35.650
And this is where
data scientists apparently spend

00:15:35.651 --> 00:15:38.325
80% of their time
before they can

00:15:38.326 --> 00:15:40.565
bring data into a form
where they can apply

00:15:40.566 --> 00:15:42.040
machine [inaudible]
in order to be able

00:15:42.041 --> 00:15:43.895
to build interesting models.

00:15:43.896 --> 00:15:47.835
Another killer application
is, quote transformations.

00:15:47.836 --> 00:15:51.000
If you want to help users or

00:15:51.001 --> 00:15:52.310
developers migrate their quote

00:15:52.311 --> 00:15:53.850
from one platform
to another one,

00:15:53.851 --> 00:15:55.380
from an old version
of a framework

00:15:55.381 --> 00:15:56.910
to a new version
of their framework.

00:15:56.911 --> 00:15:58.610
In such migration scenarios,

00:15:58.611 --> 00:16:01.090
they are apparently spending
40% percent of their time,

00:16:01.091 --> 00:16:03.025
doing their protective
quote edits.

00:16:03.026 --> 00:16:04.970
But again this is a scenario
where they can give

00:16:04.971 --> 00:16:06.770
one or two examples of
the adage they want,

00:16:06.771 --> 00:16:09.620
and the system can
automatically suggest them all

00:16:09.621 --> 00:16:10.890
of the places where they might

00:16:10.891 --> 00:16:12.750
want to do such transformations.

00:16:12.751 --> 00:16:15.030
So huge range of
applications basically.

00:16:15.031 --> 00:16:17.830
This is the thing that I want
to really emphasize here.

00:16:17.831 --> 00:16:21.860
But how did all of this
actually come about?

00:16:21.861 --> 00:16:25.100
Even starting with the fact
that I told you that you know

00:16:25.101 --> 00:16:28.390
this bubble paper that
I wrote on "Flash Fill".

00:16:28.391 --> 00:16:30.795
I spent three months,

00:16:30.796 --> 00:16:32.600
just studying the help forums.

00:16:32.601 --> 00:16:34.120
And only three weeks
coming up with

00:16:34.121 --> 00:16:35.695
an algorithm and implementing it

00:16:35.696 --> 00:16:37.035
and maybe a couple
of other weeks

00:16:37.036 --> 00:16:39.655
writing the draft of the paper.

00:16:39.656 --> 00:16:43.255
But then it took
two years to ship

00:16:43.256 --> 00:16:47.625
this product with Excel
and it was no mean feat.

00:16:47.626 --> 00:16:50.430
The first support
that I got was from

00:16:50.431 --> 00:16:52.880
my manager at
the time, Ben Zorn,

00:16:52.881 --> 00:16:55.880
who played the perfect
selfless role of

00:16:55.881 --> 00:16:57.520
ensuring that this technology

00:16:57.521 --> 00:16:59.395
actually meets its right fit.

00:16:59.396 --> 00:17:01.140
It was very hard for me to even

00:17:01.141 --> 00:17:02.640
connect with the right
sort of people in

00:17:02.641 --> 00:17:06.505
Excel in order to be able to
show them this technology.

00:17:06.506 --> 00:17:08.690
But Ben had the right sort of

00:17:08.691 --> 00:17:11.310
context and he facilitated that.

00:17:11.311 --> 00:17:14.390
We had an artist D who devoted

00:17:14.391 --> 00:17:17.005
himself for one or two years

00:17:17.006 --> 00:17:18.140
in order to be able to do this

00:17:18.141 --> 00:17:20.330
tech transfer over at Excel.

00:17:20.331 --> 00:17:23.760
We had a PM Shobana
Balakrishnan,

00:17:23.761 --> 00:17:25.145
who played the right role,

00:17:25.146 --> 00:17:26.770
in dealing with
the upper management

00:17:26.771 --> 00:17:28.885
as well as the people
on the ground,

00:17:28.886 --> 00:17:32.305
in order to get
this technology shipped out.

00:17:32.306 --> 00:17:34.310
I think the start of the show

00:17:34.311 --> 00:17:36.005
has to be the Rishabh Singh,

00:17:36.006 --> 00:17:40.990
who came here for
three different internships and

00:17:40.991 --> 00:17:43.190
when his OPT finally ran out

00:17:43.191 --> 00:17:44.260
we shipped him to India so

00:17:44.261 --> 00:17:46.500
that he can work
for us from there.

00:17:46.501 --> 00:17:50.735
He took this technology
to a completely new level.

00:17:50.736 --> 00:17:54.500
There were many interns
who followed,

00:17:54.501 --> 00:17:56.730
who worked on these different
variations that I talked

00:17:56.731 --> 00:17:59.010
about like extracting
data from locked files,

00:17:59.011 --> 00:18:00.340
extracting tabular data from

00:18:00.341 --> 00:18:02.960
semi-structured spreadsheets.

00:18:02.961 --> 00:18:05.540
Gustavo who worked on,

00:18:05.541 --> 00:18:08.085
doing quote transformations
from examples.

00:18:08.086 --> 00:18:09.935
Mark who is in the room here,

00:18:09.936 --> 00:18:13.715
who led work on programming
by natural language.

00:18:13.716 --> 00:18:17.950
There is one important character
missing on this slide.

00:18:17.951 --> 00:18:19.760
Who would you guess, what

00:18:19.761 --> 00:18:23.040
the role of that
character would be?

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:32.055
So I'm talking about
my partners here, right?

00:18:32.056 --> 00:18:36.780
&gt;&gt; Your wife. She
was the coauthor.

00:18:36.781 --> 00:18:42.140
&gt;&gt; Three internships.

00:18:42.141 --> 00:18:44.805
Two internships are
contractor position.

00:18:44.806 --> 00:18:46.415
Two-year postdoc.

00:18:46.416 --> 00:18:49.075
Almost like another
one year of funding.

00:18:49.076 --> 00:18:52.430
Where does this
funding come from?

00:18:52.431 --> 00:18:55.870
So these are the funding Gods

00:18:55.871 --> 00:18:58.280
who enabled this arrangement.

00:18:58.281 --> 00:19:00.880
So, Rico was running

00:19:00.881 --> 00:19:04.310
this impossible things
initiative at that time.

00:19:04.311 --> 00:19:07.570
And he took bet on
this technology,

00:19:07.571 --> 00:19:13.290
so that it can actually
see where it ought to be.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:19.510
So now, we invested into
so many different kinds

00:19:19.511 --> 00:19:22.885
of task domains for
program synthesis,

00:19:22.886 --> 00:19:26.480
but there was one big challenge
that developing each of

00:19:26.481 --> 00:19:31.715
these program synthesizers
was a very tedious effort.

00:19:31.716 --> 00:19:32.920
It required you to think of

00:19:32.921 --> 00:19:34.160
an algorithm from the beginning.

00:19:34.161 --> 00:19:37.020
It required you to you
to do an implementation

00:19:37.021 --> 00:19:38.590
and if you want to do
a robust implementation,

00:19:38.591 --> 00:19:42.630
you have to spend
months engineering it.

00:19:42.631 --> 00:19:46.120
And also people in academia
started rumors that,

00:19:46.121 --> 00:19:49.000
"Oh, Sumit is not only
doing practical research."

00:19:49.001 --> 00:19:52.060
So it was now time for payback.

00:19:52.061 --> 00:19:54.030
And all the environment was well

00:19:54.031 --> 00:19:56.450
set up for the second bet.

00:19:56.451 --> 00:19:58.580
Which was to develop

00:19:58.581 --> 00:20:01.440
a framework that can
facilitate the design and

00:20:01.441 --> 00:20:02.890
development of these kinds of

00:20:02.891 --> 00:20:06.810
program synthesizers for
various individual domains.

00:20:06.811 --> 00:20:09.680
So in the old Word,
we would invent

00:20:09.681 --> 00:20:11.810
a program synthesis algorithm
from scratch for

00:20:11.811 --> 00:20:14.795
a new domain and we had
worked on 20 such domains.

00:20:14.796 --> 00:20:16.590
And each of them would
be publishable in

00:20:16.591 --> 00:20:18.050
a top-tier conference and

00:20:18.051 --> 00:20:20.675
we can keep continuing
the crank there.

00:20:20.676 --> 00:20:24.650
But we want to do
something better.

00:20:24.651 --> 00:20:29.120
So we wanted to develop
a framework that

00:20:29.121 --> 00:20:30.270
would be parameterized by

00:20:30.271 --> 00:20:32.580
various domain-specific
concepts.

00:20:32.581 --> 00:20:35.565
So that it can guide
our algorithmic thinking,

00:20:35.566 --> 00:20:39.270
it can make the time to engineer

00:20:39.271 --> 00:20:43.900
a robust synthesizer from
months to a few weeks.

00:20:43.901 --> 00:20:45.370
And furthermore, this will

00:20:45.371 --> 00:20:47.110
also pave way for
doing new research.

00:20:47.111 --> 00:20:48.400
But now the new research
that will be

00:20:48.401 --> 00:20:49.820
done will actually provide

00:20:49.821 --> 00:20:51.380
value for all
different domains as

00:20:51.381 --> 00:20:54.970
opposed to being
domain-specific.

00:20:54.971 --> 00:20:59.220
So this is how the framework
actually looks like.

00:20:59.221 --> 00:21:00.855
So in the old Word,

00:21:00.856 --> 00:21:03.845
you would have a synthesizer
for each domain.

00:21:03.846 --> 00:21:06.260
Which will take as
input examples and will output

00:21:06.261 --> 00:21:10.040
the program and this is
the model insight Flash Fill is,

00:21:10.041 --> 00:21:12.760
but the first kind of
parameterization that we

00:21:12.761 --> 00:21:14.875
did here was that instead of

00:21:14.876 --> 00:21:16.230
having the search algorithm to

00:21:16.231 --> 00:21:17.550
be very domain-specific and

00:21:17.551 --> 00:21:19.050
having a separate
search algorithm

00:21:19.051 --> 00:21:20.640
for each different domain,

00:21:20.641 --> 00:21:23.630
we parameterized
this search algorithm with

00:21:23.631 --> 00:21:25.540
the underlying
domain-specific language

00:21:25.541 --> 00:21:27.785
in which we wanted to
synthesize programs.

00:21:27.786 --> 00:21:30.975
So now to develop a synthesizer
for a different domain,

00:21:30.976 --> 00:21:35.380
you can plug in a separate
domain-specific language.

00:21:35.381 --> 00:21:39.160
Another interesting
point here is

00:21:39.161 --> 00:21:43.465
that examples are a very
ambiguous form of user's intent.

00:21:43.466 --> 00:21:45.450
When you give me
one or two examples,

00:21:45.451 --> 00:21:46.825
the number of programs

00:21:46.826 --> 00:21:48.360
in my domain-specific
language that

00:21:48.361 --> 00:21:52.065
match those examples is
often 10 to the power 50.

00:21:52.066 --> 00:21:54.680
So we need to pick a program

00:21:54.681 --> 00:21:57.040
that we believe matches
that user's intent.

00:21:57.041 --> 00:21:59.160
And if we don't get this right,

00:21:59.161 --> 00:22:00.965
Excel will not see Flash Fill.

00:22:00.966 --> 00:22:02.820
In fact, they demanded that for

00:22:02.821 --> 00:22:05.420
most simple tasks we ought
to read the mind of the user

00:22:05.421 --> 00:22:07.510
from just one input/output
example because otherwise

00:22:07.511 --> 00:22:08.600
people would lose trust in

00:22:08.601 --> 00:22:09.920
this technology. They
would not use it.

00:22:09.921 --> 00:22:12.030
It will come up with
funny interpretations.

00:22:12.031 --> 00:22:14.180
And the ranking
algorithm that we used

00:22:14.181 --> 00:22:16.205
was intertwined with
the search algorithm.

00:22:16.206 --> 00:22:17.570
But this was a good opportunity

00:22:17.571 --> 00:22:19.340
to separate the two
things together.

00:22:19.341 --> 00:22:22.010
So now the search algorithm
returns a program set,

00:22:22.011 --> 00:22:23.690
the set of all programs that

00:22:23.691 --> 00:22:25.700
match the examples
and now we have

00:22:25.701 --> 00:22:27.670
a separate ranker which

00:22:27.671 --> 00:22:28.720
can take the program set and

00:22:28.721 --> 00:22:30.265
introduce a rank program set.

00:22:30.266 --> 00:22:32.870
And furthermore, this ranker
can be parameterized by

00:22:32.871 --> 00:22:35.905
different kinds of
ranking functions or features.

00:22:35.906 --> 00:22:39.055
We can also build
interactivity in this loop.

00:22:39.056 --> 00:22:40.910
We can actually
guide the user in

00:22:40.911 --> 00:22:44.475
deciding if they need to
provide us more examples or not.

00:22:44.476 --> 00:22:46.320
And once we believe

00:22:46.321 --> 00:22:47.870
that they might have
a correct program,

00:22:47.871 --> 00:22:49.530
we can show them the program in

00:22:49.531 --> 00:22:51.590
our domain-specific language
which can then

00:22:51.591 --> 00:22:55.370
be translated to a new language
of choice like Python,

00:22:55.371 --> 00:22:57.125
R, C Sharp or Java.

00:22:57.126 --> 00:22:59.890
So this is what the overall
structure architecture looks

00:22:59.891 --> 00:23:03.230
like. But how did we get here?

00:23:03.231 --> 00:23:04.530
&gt;&gt; Question.

00:23:04.531 --> 00:23:04.880
&gt; Yeah.

00:23:04.881 --> 00:23:06.010
&gt;&gt; So for the user input,

00:23:06.011 --> 00:23:07.840
you are exposing
it to the user in

00:23:07.841 --> 00:23:10.170
the domain-specific language and

00:23:10.171 --> 00:23:11.940
the other motivation you have is

00:23:11.941 --> 00:23:14.610
the user who doesn't
know programming, right?

00:23:14.611 --> 00:23:16.135
&gt;&gt; Yes. It is a good question.

00:23:16.136 --> 00:23:19.090
So the end user only interacts
with the red color hair.

00:23:19.091 --> 00:23:19.620
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:23:19.621 --> 00:23:21.530
&gt;&gt; The blue color is done by

00:23:21.531 --> 00:23:25.740
researchers or
advanced developers like us.

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:33.930
So Vu Le had
developed this system

00:23:33.931 --> 00:23:36.940
called Flash Extract which

00:23:36.941 --> 00:23:40.960
would extract tabular data
from log files.

00:23:40.961 --> 00:23:44.690
And we wanted to also

00:23:44.691 --> 00:23:46.500
extend this work to be able

00:23:46.501 --> 00:23:48.550
to extract data from web pages,

00:23:48.551 --> 00:23:51.970
extract data from
semi-structure documents.

00:23:51.971 --> 00:23:55.280
And then he ended up doing
another implementation

00:23:55.281 --> 00:23:58.065
of Flash Extract for log files,

00:23:58.066 --> 00:23:59.210
so that it becomes general

00:23:59.211 --> 00:24:01.120
enough that we can also extract,

00:24:01.121 --> 00:24:03.275
use the same framework
to extract data from

00:24:03.276 --> 00:24:07.530
the web pages,
from spreadsheets.

00:24:07.531 --> 00:24:10.970
And this resulted
in some kind of

00:24:10.971 --> 00:24:15.160
generalization and a step
towards this framework.

00:24:15.161 --> 00:24:20.030
We had another intern
Dileep Kini who worked

00:24:20.031 --> 00:24:22.600
on extending Flash Fill

00:24:22.601 --> 00:24:24.655
to doing much more
complicated tasks.

00:24:24.656 --> 00:24:27.000
So he experimented with
new interaction models,

00:24:27.001 --> 00:24:30.765
he experimented with
new synthesis techniques.

00:24:30.766 --> 00:24:33.525
And I would say it
was a mild success.

00:24:33.526 --> 00:24:35.615
It resulted in a paper,

00:24:35.616 --> 00:24:38.820
but did not make as much dent
as we had hoped for.

00:24:38.821 --> 00:24:40.545
But these were
kind of fine steps,

00:24:40.546 --> 00:24:43.465
two fine steps
towards the process.

00:24:43.466 --> 00:24:46.210
Around the same time, I had

00:24:46.211 --> 00:24:49.805
an intern Alex Polozov who
is actually in the room,

00:24:49.806 --> 00:24:52.840
who was being randomized by
me on many different kinds of

00:24:52.841 --> 00:24:53.940
projects and he was watching

00:24:53.941 --> 00:24:56.235
this thing happening
from the sidelines.

00:24:56.236 --> 00:24:58.720
And then he came to me and
he said, "You know Sumit,

00:24:58.721 --> 00:24:59.720
I can solve the problem

00:24:59.721 --> 00:25:02.055
that you are looking
to solve here."

00:25:02.056 --> 00:25:04.220
But he was not clear

00:25:04.221 --> 00:25:05.630
whether he would want
to solve it or not.

00:25:05.631 --> 00:25:06.790
How will this actually work out

00:25:06.791 --> 00:25:08.570
because he was a student at

00:25:08.571 --> 00:25:10.690
UDub and solving this problem

00:25:10.691 --> 00:25:13.125
really require a big dedication.

00:25:13.126 --> 00:25:15.580
I convinced him several weeks

00:25:15.581 --> 00:25:17.370
and then finally he decided
that he will actually

00:25:17.371 --> 00:25:21.280
be doing his PhD at
Microsoft Research,

00:25:21.281 --> 00:25:25.040
working full-time on
this particular project.

00:25:25.041 --> 00:25:28.550
And so he did, he actually
solved this problem.

00:25:28.551 --> 00:25:34.895
And I think there is a beautiful
paper that he authored.

00:25:34.896 --> 00:25:38.140
But while he was focused on

00:25:38.141 --> 00:25:41.410
developing the nuts and
bolts of the framework,

00:25:41.411 --> 00:25:43.960
we acquired Vu to

00:25:43.961 --> 00:25:47.800
reimplement Flash Extract
yet another time.

00:25:47.801 --> 00:25:51.405
So first he implemented
Flash Extract for log files.

00:25:51.406 --> 00:25:53.640
Actually, that was
a second implementation of

00:25:53.641 --> 00:25:55.410
Flash Extract for log files
because it was one

00:25:55.411 --> 00:25:57.580
other person Herman Venter
who had developed

00:25:57.581 --> 00:25:58.980
the first implementation but

00:25:58.981 --> 00:26:01.405
unfortunately he left
the company after a year.

00:26:01.406 --> 00:26:04.495
Then Vu developed
Flash Extract the second time.

00:26:04.496 --> 00:26:06.780
Vu implemented Flash Extract

00:26:06.781 --> 00:26:08.160
the third time so that we can

00:26:08.161 --> 00:26:12.210
generalize it not just to
log files but to other formats.

00:26:12.211 --> 00:26:15.835
And then, he provided
great service to Alex by

00:26:15.836 --> 00:26:17.560
reimplementing Flash Extract in

00:26:17.561 --> 00:26:20.120
the most general framework
that we now have.

00:26:20.121 --> 00:26:23.365
So four times implementation
of Flash Extract.

00:26:23.366 --> 00:26:25.160
And not only that
other interns who were

00:26:25.161 --> 00:26:27.030
also around or who
could participate

00:26:27.031 --> 00:26:28.870
tried to reimplement
what they had done in

00:26:28.871 --> 00:26:33.020
the framework that
Alex proposed.

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:37.570
So just imagine the amount

00:26:37.571 --> 00:26:39.495
of investment that
is going into this.

00:26:39.496 --> 00:26:42.465
Implementing Flash Extract
the fourth time.

00:26:42.466 --> 00:26:45.275
What business value
is that adding?

00:26:45.276 --> 00:26:48.440
And who on earth would
fund that effort?

00:26:48.441 --> 00:26:50.700
Rico, I gave a lot of you know

00:26:50.701 --> 00:26:53.200
probably too much
credit last time when I

00:26:53.201 --> 00:26:55.000
said that you funded

00:26:55.001 --> 00:26:56.510
Flash Fill and Flash
Extract because

00:26:56.511 --> 00:26:58.740
any sane person would want to
throw money at that, right?

00:26:58.741 --> 00:27:01.080
It's so cool, it just looks
so cool from this concept.

00:27:01.081 --> 00:27:04.175
But who would put money on this?

00:27:04.176 --> 00:27:08.270
This is the real credit
that actually Rico gets.

00:27:08.271 --> 00:27:10.530
Again, months of internship,

00:27:10.531 --> 00:27:14.570
paying for the stipend of
Alex for the entire year,

00:27:14.571 --> 00:27:17.470
paying his tuition,
it is quite expensive

00:27:17.471 --> 00:27:19.170
but this is the place where

00:27:19.171 --> 00:27:21.455
Rico really put
his foot forward.

00:27:21.456 --> 00:27:23.560
And I'm not sure if he trusted,

00:27:23.561 --> 00:27:25.150
he put a bet on the project

00:27:25.151 --> 00:27:27.255
but probably he also
put a bet on me.

00:27:27.256 --> 00:27:29.820
So it was his bet on

00:27:29.821 --> 00:27:34.290
both the technology
as well as people.

00:27:34.340 --> 00:27:39.030
Now, what was
the next problem here?

00:27:39.031 --> 00:27:43.525
So I have to tell you
that Alex is a talent.

00:27:43.526 --> 00:27:47.830
He's one of the best engineers
that I've known and

00:27:47.831 --> 00:27:50.070
he could deal with
the sophisticated theory

00:27:50.071 --> 00:27:51.630
that is there inside this paper.

00:27:51.631 --> 00:27:55.045
It's hard to find gems
like him, absolutely hard.

00:27:55.046 --> 00:27:56.790
I cannot not just
manufacture new

00:27:56.791 --> 00:27:58.730
Alex's and put them together.

00:27:58.731 --> 00:28:01.090
And also he needs
to be left alone to

00:28:01.091 --> 00:28:04.630
decide his own paths
and directions in life.

00:28:04.631 --> 00:28:06.920
So how can I carry forward

00:28:06.921 --> 00:28:08.950
this mission where I really need

00:28:08.951 --> 00:28:13.410
to take this framework and
make it production quality.

00:28:13.411 --> 00:28:16.910
If we just continue to
depend upon in interns,

00:28:16.911 --> 00:28:18.780
this is not going to work out.

00:28:18.781 --> 00:28:20.920
Because interns
are going to leave,

00:28:20.921 --> 00:28:22.210
they can come for
three internships,

00:28:22.211 --> 00:28:23.450
they can again
extend the contract

00:28:23.451 --> 00:28:24.820
for another six months.

00:28:24.821 --> 00:28:28.020
We can continue to get
angel funding from Rico,

00:28:28.021 --> 00:28:30.960
but this model is
not going to sustain.

00:28:30.961 --> 00:28:34.170
So something has to really
change and this brings me to

00:28:34.171 --> 00:28:36.780
the third bet which
is about my belief

00:28:36.781 --> 00:28:40.400
off putting research and
engineering together.

00:28:40.401 --> 00:28:42.680
So if you recall the success of

00:28:42.681 --> 00:28:45.630
Flash Fill relied on

00:28:45.631 --> 00:28:48.570
two years of engagement
with the Excel team,

00:28:48.571 --> 00:28:52.010
where we had built
a research prototype and

00:28:52.011 --> 00:28:54.410
the Excel team took it

00:28:54.411 --> 00:28:58.010
and copied it
line-by-line into C++.

00:28:58.011 --> 00:29:00.870
So our C Sharp code was
copied line-by-line into C++.

00:29:00.871 --> 00:29:02.970
And if they had
any performance issues

00:29:02.971 --> 00:29:04.635
or some bugs that needed to fix,

00:29:04.636 --> 00:29:06.220
they would get back
to us, we would fix

00:29:06.221 --> 00:29:08.600
it in our code and then
they would take it.

00:29:08.601 --> 00:29:10.750
But we want to do
more such things

00:29:10.751 --> 00:29:12.540
and you want to
accelerate them, right?

00:29:12.541 --> 00:29:14.960
So using this model of what we

00:29:14.961 --> 00:29:17.455
call Tech Transfer is
not going to work here.

00:29:17.456 --> 00:29:22.020
We have to build and
deliver that tech ourselves.

00:29:22.021 --> 00:29:25.805
&gt;&gt; Did you consider

00:29:25.806 --> 00:29:29.390
the option to open
source and therefore get

00:29:29.391 --> 00:29:36.100
more traction for the software
that you were building?

00:29:36.101 --> 00:29:37.870
&gt;&gt; That's an
interesting question

00:29:37.871 --> 00:29:39.480
and my feel is
that time is still

00:29:39.481 --> 00:29:41.140
not right before we

00:29:41.141 --> 00:29:43.070
get there because we're
still in the early stages.

00:29:43.071 --> 00:29:44.690
I think the project
needs to be matured up to

00:29:44.691 --> 00:29:46.200
a certain level when

00:29:46.201 --> 00:29:50.560
open sourcing will
start adding value.

00:29:50.561 --> 00:29:56.260
Also my role up until now

00:29:56.261 --> 00:29:59.070
was more of an
individual contributor.

00:29:59.071 --> 00:30:03.345
Of course I was working
with many collaborators,

00:30:03.346 --> 00:30:05.630
I was involved with
20 projects in the year.

00:30:05.631 --> 00:30:07.620
And in one year, I was
working with a total of

00:30:07.621 --> 00:30:10.785
50 people on those 20 projects.
So I was not alone.

00:30:10.786 --> 00:30:12.420
But I was still,
what I will call

00:30:12.421 --> 00:30:14.460
an individual contributor.

00:30:14.461 --> 00:30:18.390
I was splitting my time
into multiple projects,

00:30:18.391 --> 00:30:20.370
spending five percent of
my time on this project,

00:30:20.371 --> 00:30:22.200
10 percent of my time on
this project and these

00:30:22.201 --> 00:30:24.810
were all disparate projects.

00:30:24.811 --> 00:30:27.490
Some may be loosely connected.

00:30:27.491 --> 00:30:30.680
But what we needed was a team,

00:30:30.681 --> 00:30:33.310
which will have a shared mission

00:30:33.311 --> 00:30:35.055
and which will
actually work together.

00:30:35.056 --> 00:30:37.525
So I sometimes joke and
explain my situation,

00:30:37.526 --> 00:30:40.460
this past situation if
individual contributor as,

00:30:40.461 --> 00:30:43.625
I was having multiple
girlfriends at the same time.

00:30:43.626 --> 00:30:45.945
Which was of course
a very thrilling situation.

00:30:45.946 --> 00:30:47.710
But now, with a team,

00:30:47.711 --> 00:30:49.675
it is like being
settled in a family,

00:30:49.676 --> 00:30:51.650
where everyone has
the same mission

00:30:51.651 --> 00:30:54.595
and working towards
the same thing.

00:30:54.596 --> 00:30:58.240
And another thing that got me

00:30:58.241 --> 00:31:00.660
quite inspired from one
of Peter Lee's slides

00:31:00.661 --> 00:31:03.130
on different quadrants is

00:31:03.131 --> 00:31:06.220
the appreciation of two
different styles of research.

00:31:06.221 --> 00:31:09.550
One of which he called
long-term Blue-Sky research

00:31:09.551 --> 00:31:13.125
and the other thing was
shot-term mission-focused.

00:31:13.126 --> 00:31:15.600
And I used to think that,

00:31:15.601 --> 00:31:17.630
at some point of time,

00:31:17.631 --> 00:31:20.120
I need to be in one
of those quadrants.

00:31:20.121 --> 00:31:22.060
But this is the assumption
that I started

00:31:22.061 --> 00:31:26.720
challenging and now what
I believe is that we

00:31:26.721 --> 00:31:29.510
ought to fund
Blue-Sky research by using

00:31:29.511 --> 00:31:31.810
mission-focused deliverables
and we need to find

00:31:31.811 --> 00:31:35.950
synergies between
these two goals.

00:31:35.951 --> 00:31:38.600
So how would this research and

00:31:38.601 --> 00:31:41.620
engineering be put together?

00:31:41.621 --> 00:31:44.440
So here is a slide
that I have try to

00:31:44.441 --> 00:31:47.355
capture some of
my thoughts here.

00:31:47.356 --> 00:31:51.030
So first, I notice that there is

00:31:51.031 --> 00:31:53.070
a quite a parallelism between

00:31:53.071 --> 00:31:56.800
the research world and
the engineering world.

00:31:56.801 --> 00:31:59.980
And both those worlds
have appreciation for

00:31:59.981 --> 00:32:02.805
long-term processes as well as

00:32:02.806 --> 00:32:07.745
short-term value proposition
that they offer.

00:32:07.746 --> 00:32:12.625
So work like Flash Fill will
actually fit in this bucket.

00:32:12.626 --> 00:32:15.635
Where our goal is to work
on the next shiny thing,

00:32:15.636 --> 00:32:19.230
write a research paper,
make a quick impact.

00:32:19.231 --> 00:32:22.095
They work on Flash meta.

00:32:22.096 --> 00:32:24.605
This is our framework
that Alex built,

00:32:24.606 --> 00:32:27.175
will fall in this bucket here.

00:32:27.176 --> 00:32:28.960
And normally, what
we would do is,

00:32:28.961 --> 00:32:36.100
we will do multiple instantiations
first like Flash Fill,

00:32:36.101 --> 00:32:38.020
Flash Extract, Flash Normalize,

00:32:38.021 --> 00:32:39.300
Flash Relate and will then

00:32:39.301 --> 00:32:41.030
try to generalize
them into a framework.

00:32:41.031 --> 00:32:42.330
This is the old World, this is

00:32:42.331 --> 00:32:44.535
the old typical academic World.

00:32:44.536 --> 00:32:46.460
But now for a change,

00:32:46.461 --> 00:32:49.740
what if we productize
the framework and

00:32:49.741 --> 00:32:51.560
then we start building

00:32:51.561 --> 00:32:53.145
innovation on top
of that framework.

00:32:53.146 --> 00:32:57.145
And this is a new world which
will speed-up innovation.

00:32:57.146 --> 00:33:00.395
Also by trying to do something

00:33:00.396 --> 00:33:03.150
cool and then trying
to figure it out,

00:33:03.151 --> 00:33:05.660
what kind of consumers
or customers can have

00:33:05.661 --> 00:33:08.375
a impact or in which product
can I put my technology.

00:33:08.376 --> 00:33:10.420
That's a very fine way
of doing things.

00:33:10.421 --> 00:33:11.940
But I think in the new world,

00:33:11.941 --> 00:33:13.230
what I would like
to do things is

00:33:13.231 --> 00:33:14.250
completely the other way around.

00:33:14.251 --> 00:33:15.560
Where I would first
like to connect with

00:33:15.561 --> 00:33:17.270
customers and try to

00:33:17.271 --> 00:33:19.000
understand what are some
of the relevant problems.

00:33:19.001 --> 00:33:20.430
So that I don't waste
my time trying to

00:33:20.431 --> 00:33:22.440
solve problems which
are not as relevant.

00:33:22.441 --> 00:33:24.740
And both problems are equally
intellectually interesting.

00:33:24.741 --> 00:33:26.300
But if I have a choice,
I would rather pick

00:33:26.301 --> 00:33:28.600
something that is more relevant.

00:33:28.601 --> 00:33:30.840
And furthermore, if I set up

00:33:30.841 --> 00:33:33.245
my engineering processes right,

00:33:33.246 --> 00:33:36.270
then all of
these three quadrants

00:33:36.271 --> 00:33:38.710
are going to actually have
a push onto innovation.

00:33:38.711 --> 00:33:40.465
So even if I end up spending

00:33:40.466 --> 00:33:43.100
a fraction of my time
in this quadrant,

00:33:43.101 --> 00:33:44.620
the fact that I've invested here

00:33:44.621 --> 00:33:46.760
and these other quadrants
is actually going

00:33:46.761 --> 00:33:48.240
to push and accelerate

00:33:48.241 --> 00:33:51.305
the development of new ideas
and their delivery.

00:33:51.306 --> 00:33:53.630
So this is what we also

00:33:53.631 --> 00:33:56.575
see with the interns
that we sometimes get.

00:33:56.576 --> 00:33:58.505
So I had experience where,

00:33:58.506 --> 00:34:00.605
if I get a very smart student

00:34:00.606 --> 00:34:02.945
but who is not that
great at engineering.

00:34:02.946 --> 00:34:04.880
They actually sometimes struggle

00:34:04.881 --> 00:34:06.930
because with the limited time
that they have,

00:34:06.931 --> 00:34:09.295
they're able to
tryout only few ideas.

00:34:09.296 --> 00:34:10.800
But someone who's
probably not as

00:34:10.801 --> 00:34:13.510
good but a much
strong engineer is

00:34:13.511 --> 00:34:15.980
sometimes more successful
because they're able to try

00:34:15.981 --> 00:34:20.245
out many more ideas in
the same amount of time.

00:34:20.246 --> 00:34:24.640
So this is the kind of
a structure that I wanted,

00:34:24.641 --> 00:34:26.430
where engineers and researchers

00:34:26.431 --> 00:34:28.555
would be working
synergistically.

00:34:28.556 --> 00:34:31.530
And this is the team
that I now have,

00:34:31.531 --> 00:34:34.810
where the goal of
researchers is not to

00:34:34.811 --> 00:34:36.200
develop prototypes
and throw it to

00:34:36.201 --> 00:34:38.325
engineers for them
to productize.

00:34:38.326 --> 00:34:40.570
So researchers have to do

00:34:40.571 --> 00:34:43.090
product quality implementation
of their ideas.

00:34:43.091 --> 00:34:45.100
But I do acknowledge
the fact that,

00:34:45.101 --> 00:34:47.315
not everyone is like Alex.

00:34:47.316 --> 00:34:49.390
People are not trained,

00:34:49.391 --> 00:34:51.250
to be great software engineers.

00:34:51.251 --> 00:34:53.710
But as long as the engineers
on the team are

00:34:53.711 --> 00:34:56.355
willing to give the
researchers guidance,

00:34:56.356 --> 00:34:59.105
on how to do right
software engineering.

00:34:59.106 --> 00:35:01.020
And as long as
researchers are open to

00:35:01.021 --> 00:35:02.945
picking up on it,
it will work out.

00:35:02.946 --> 00:35:04.270
And this is the arrangement that

00:35:04.271 --> 00:35:06.525
I have on my team right now.

00:35:06.526 --> 00:35:09.825
And furthermore, it's teamwork.

00:35:09.826 --> 00:35:12.500
So while every researcher
on the team has

00:35:12.501 --> 00:35:15.925
their ownership of
some important capability,

00:35:15.926 --> 00:35:17.690
all these capabilities
are coming

00:35:17.691 --> 00:35:19.875
together towards
a common mission.

00:35:19.876 --> 00:35:21.605
It's not like
everyone is trying to

00:35:21.606 --> 00:35:23.825
create their own islands
of excellence.

00:35:23.826 --> 00:35:25.160
So as a Research Manager,

00:35:25.161 --> 00:35:27.030
my responsibility
is to make sure that

00:35:27.031 --> 00:35:28.050
every researcher in the team

00:35:28.051 --> 00:35:30.395
shines, with whatever they do.

00:35:30.396 --> 00:35:33.110
But it also all
coherently fits together

00:35:33.111 --> 00:35:37.980
as part of one
single big mission.

00:35:37.981 --> 00:35:41.370
So many people think,

00:35:41.371 --> 00:35:45.930
that I'm running a product team
or an engineering team.

00:35:45.931 --> 00:35:48.590
This is completely not true.

00:35:48.591 --> 00:35:50.640
I'm running as strong

00:35:50.641 --> 00:35:53.860
a research team as you might
find inside this building.

00:35:53.861 --> 00:35:56.815
The only reason why I moved out,

00:35:56.816 --> 00:35:59.275
was because I did not want to be

00:35:59.276 --> 00:36:02.890
a burden on MSR or
more of a drain on MSR.

00:36:02.891 --> 00:36:06.790
I wanted to leave
the angel investors like

00:36:06.791 --> 00:36:12.440
Rico free to fund
new entrepreneurs like Sumit.

00:36:12.441 --> 00:36:15.330
This is the kind of model which

00:36:15.331 --> 00:36:18.220
I believe is more sustainable.

00:36:18.221 --> 00:36:22.405
This is the message that
I want to spread around,

00:36:22.406 --> 00:36:29.555
that product groups should
fund research not just

00:36:29.556 --> 00:36:33.940
as a philanthropic activity

00:36:33.941 --> 00:36:37.640
but it can really
increase their business,

00:36:37.641 --> 00:36:38.955
it can really bring them

00:36:38.956 --> 00:36:42.125
distinguish value
to their business.

00:36:42.126 --> 00:36:45.475
And if we start
thinking like this,

00:36:45.476 --> 00:36:47.920
there's a huge amount
of money in this world.

00:36:47.921 --> 00:36:49.470
Now, outside of this small

00:36:49.471 --> 00:36:51.830
little organization
that we live in.

00:36:51.831 --> 00:36:54.830
And it is inside this
outside world that we should

00:36:54.831 --> 00:36:58.755
be seeking real funding that
goes beyond angel investing.

00:36:58.756 --> 00:37:03.460
So this is a message that I
really want to share here.

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:08.875
So now last the final bet,

00:37:08.876 --> 00:37:10.850
and this is about

00:37:10.851 --> 00:37:14.445
my belief in
Cross-Disciplinary Research.

00:37:14.446 --> 00:37:17.235
The initial bug actually hit me,

00:37:17.236 --> 00:37:19.165
when I was working
with the Nebosh.

00:37:19.166 --> 00:37:21.450
Who was part of the reason

00:37:21.451 --> 00:37:23.280
why I even joined
Microsoft Research,

00:37:23.281 --> 00:37:25.160
Redmond in the first place.

00:37:25.161 --> 00:37:27.010
Because we saw
potential off applying

00:37:27.011 --> 00:37:29.285
machine learning for
program synthesis.

00:37:29.286 --> 00:37:31.735
But sorry Nebosh,
I didn't followup

00:37:31.736 --> 00:37:34.605
too much on that after
the initial project.

00:37:34.606 --> 00:37:39.335
But more recently, this
has come to be very true.

00:37:39.336 --> 00:37:43.125
So Cross-Disciplinary
Research is hard.

00:37:43.126 --> 00:37:47.430
For many reasons,
it is difficult

00:37:47.431 --> 00:37:49.880
to explain to
the other community

00:37:49.881 --> 00:37:52.295
that you have done
something meaningful.

00:37:52.296 --> 00:37:54.610
It is difficult to receive
funding in that thing,

00:37:54.611 --> 00:37:56.650
you're not really
a expert in that area.

00:37:56.651 --> 00:37:58.195
So what you typically do,

00:37:58.196 --> 00:38:00.415
right? You might just ignore it.

00:38:00.416 --> 00:38:02.490
You may ignore it
for various reasons.

00:38:02.491 --> 00:38:03.690
One reason you might ignore it

00:38:03.691 --> 00:38:05.295
is that you really believe that,

00:38:05.296 --> 00:38:07.130
your community has
the right tools and

00:38:07.131 --> 00:38:10.730
techniques to take
a stab at that problem.

00:38:10.731 --> 00:38:17.140
Or you might ignore it
because you simply don't care.

00:38:17.141 --> 00:38:21.615
It's like Ostrich hiding
its head inside the sand,

00:38:21.616 --> 00:38:25.020
or the other option that
you have to remain in

00:38:25.021 --> 00:38:28.850
awe of the other kind
of technology.

00:38:28.851 --> 00:38:32.930
And the change that I'm now
imbibing in my new world,

00:38:32.931 --> 00:38:36.910
is the belief that the third
wave of AI will actually

00:38:36.911 --> 00:38:38.690
combine the techniques from

00:38:38.691 --> 00:38:41.185
my area which is that
of formal methods,

00:38:41.186 --> 00:38:43.370
logical reasoning
techniques, and

00:38:43.371 --> 00:38:45.990
from that of the machine
learning community,

00:38:45.991 --> 00:38:49.785
all the statistical
learning that is their.

00:38:49.786 --> 00:38:52.920
But how would I go about

00:38:52.921 --> 00:38:56.460
doing this
Cross-disciplinary Research.

00:38:56.461 --> 00:38:58.625
I'm not an expert in this area.

00:38:58.626 --> 00:39:00.990
I really need to find the right

00:39:00.991 --> 00:39:04.240
empathetic expert
who is willing to be

00:39:04.241 --> 00:39:06.100
a partner because

00:39:06.101 --> 00:39:07.980
simple collaborations
for the sake of writing

00:39:07.981 --> 00:39:09.850
a paper are not going to help

00:39:09.851 --> 00:39:13.230
and push this thing
much farther.

00:39:13.231 --> 00:39:15.850
So let me actually
explain to you

00:39:15.851 --> 00:39:17.525
what are the opportunities for,

00:39:17.526 --> 00:39:19.150
why I believe that combining

00:39:19.151 --> 00:39:21.260
logic and machine learning is

00:39:21.261 --> 00:39:23.310
quite important for creating

00:39:23.311 --> 00:39:26.880
many different kinds
of AI software.

00:39:26.881 --> 00:39:31.160
So, here's some
intelligent piece

00:39:31.161 --> 00:39:34.815
of some software that
behaves intelligently,

00:39:34.816 --> 00:39:37.710
and three instances of
such pieces would be

00:39:37.711 --> 00:39:39.620
the three components
that I showed you

00:39:39.621 --> 00:39:41.550
in this framework for
program synthesis.

00:39:41.551 --> 00:39:42.980
One was the search algorithm,

00:39:42.981 --> 00:39:46.245
the ranking algorithm, and
the disambiguation algorithm.

00:39:46.246 --> 00:39:49.580
So such an intelligent component

00:39:49.581 --> 00:39:54.180
is often composed of
two different pieces:

00:39:54.181 --> 00:39:58.580
logical strategies or algorithms
that we understand very

00:39:58.581 --> 00:40:00.990
well and something that I would

00:40:00.991 --> 00:40:04.555
like to call
creative heuristics.

00:40:04.556 --> 00:40:08.430
If I further drill down into
the creative heuristics,

00:40:08.431 --> 00:40:09.900
at least another code base,

00:40:09.901 --> 00:40:11.930
we find that it is made up

00:40:11.931 --> 00:40:13.870
of various kinds of

00:40:13.871 --> 00:40:16.310
features and some weights
over those features.

00:40:16.311 --> 00:40:17.910
For instance there is a constant

00:40:17.911 --> 00:40:20.215
0.8 being used in my code base.

00:40:20.216 --> 00:40:22.800
So, I asked a researcher,
"Why did we used 0.8?"

00:40:22.801 --> 00:40:26.045
The answer is it just
seems to work well.

00:40:26.046 --> 00:40:29.090
But in the new world,

00:40:29.091 --> 00:40:30.870
it would be fantastic

00:40:30.871 --> 00:40:34.200
if developers or researchers
focus their time on

00:40:34.201 --> 00:40:36.960
identifying the right features
and writing what is

00:40:36.961 --> 00:40:40.825
a well-understood knowledge and

00:40:40.826 --> 00:40:42.610
leave the modern
building exercise

00:40:42.611 --> 00:40:45.590
to a machine learning
backed runtime.

00:40:45.591 --> 00:40:47.710
This can help us come up

00:40:47.711 --> 00:40:50.260
with not only better models
and make us more

00:40:50.261 --> 00:40:52.830
effective but it
can also lead to

00:40:52.831 --> 00:40:56.265
these systems being
adaptive and personalized.

00:40:56.266 --> 00:40:58.310
So just imagine, when Flash Fill

00:40:58.311 --> 00:41:00.610
is running on
different user's machines.

00:41:00.611 --> 00:41:02.340
If a user is dealing with

00:41:02.341 --> 00:41:03.890
the same kind of data
over and over again,

00:41:03.891 --> 00:41:05.850
then instead of having to
give two or three examples,

00:41:05.851 --> 00:41:06.930
the system can understand their

00:41:06.931 --> 00:41:08.550
intent from just one example.

00:41:08.551 --> 00:41:12.100
In some cases even 0.1 examples
are zero examples, right?

00:41:12.101 --> 00:41:14.180
And I can formalize that
maybe if you're interested,

00:41:14.181 --> 00:41:16.220
you can talk to
me after the talk.

00:41:16.221 --> 00:41:18.760
So this kind of
adaptivity would be

00:41:18.761 --> 00:41:21.520
possible only if we are using

00:41:21.521 --> 00:41:24.370
some automated way to fine-tune

00:41:24.371 --> 00:41:25.770
the creative heuristics or

00:41:25.771 --> 00:41:26.990
the ranking strategies that are

00:41:26.991 --> 00:41:28.995
inside underneath the software.

00:41:28.996 --> 00:41:34.195
So this kind of division
would facilitate that.

00:41:34.196 --> 00:41:37.560
So I can go a little bit
more into this detail

00:41:37.561 --> 00:41:39.940
for each of the three components

00:41:39.941 --> 00:41:42.825
that I showed you in
the Program Synthesis framework.

00:41:42.826 --> 00:41:45.475
So if you recall, there
was a search algorithm

00:41:45.476 --> 00:41:48.840
that was parameterized by
a domain-specific language.

00:41:48.841 --> 00:41:51.050
And, the domain-specific
language is usually

00:41:51.051 --> 00:41:53.610
specified as some rules
in a grammar.

00:41:53.611 --> 00:41:57.230
And, we have a fancy search
strategy that we used

00:41:57.231 --> 00:41:59.200
to explore that
grammar in order to

00:41:59.201 --> 00:42:01.610
figured out the program that
satisfies the user's intent.

00:42:01.611 --> 00:42:03.670
And there are lots of
heuristics that we use

00:42:03.671 --> 00:42:04.840
actually in order to make this

00:42:04.841 --> 00:42:07.120
process scalable and robust.

00:42:07.121 --> 00:42:12.035
Which grammar production
to try first or to not try.

00:42:12.036 --> 00:42:15.200
Which disjunctive,
which sub-goal

00:42:15.201 --> 00:42:18.645
from a disjunction of sub-goals
to explore when we are

00:42:18.646 --> 00:42:21.850
trying to make our progress
towards the goal?

00:42:21.851 --> 00:42:24.150
And each of these
decisions can actually

00:42:24.151 --> 00:42:25.860
be learned from large amounts of

00:42:25.861 --> 00:42:27.180
data that we have

00:42:27.181 --> 00:42:29.675
and if we use the
right machine learning,

00:42:29.676 --> 00:42:32.370
modeling, and learning gear.

00:42:32.371 --> 00:42:36.190
Here is another example
of Program Ranking.

00:42:36.191 --> 00:42:38.700
So we have many
programs that match

00:42:38.701 --> 00:42:40.250
the users input/output examples

00:42:40.251 --> 00:42:42.000
but we need to choose
one of those programs.

00:42:42.001 --> 00:42:44.090
So we need to have
techniques that can rank

00:42:44.091 --> 00:42:47.510
different programs and
this ranking is based upon

00:42:47.511 --> 00:42:49.620
syntactic features
of the program

00:42:49.621 --> 00:42:51.380
or even features of the outputs

00:42:51.381 --> 00:42:53.560
produced by two or
three different programs

00:42:53.561 --> 00:42:56.050
on a given set of inputs.

00:42:56.051 --> 00:42:57.800
Now, what weights do you use

00:42:57.801 --> 00:42:59.235
for these different features?

00:42:59.236 --> 00:43:00.920
And this is again
something that can be

00:43:00.921 --> 00:43:02.640
learned from machine learning.

00:43:02.641 --> 00:43:04.035
In fact, some features are

00:43:04.036 --> 00:43:06.110
more machine learning
than logical like,

00:43:06.111 --> 00:43:09.505
"Is this string
the name of a person?"

00:43:09.506 --> 00:43:14.185
And in the disambiguation box
that I showed you,

00:43:14.186 --> 00:43:16.360
we need to communicate

00:43:16.361 --> 00:43:18.480
some actionable information
to the user so

00:43:18.481 --> 00:43:20.280
that the user can
decide if they need to

00:43:20.281 --> 00:43:23.730
provide more examples
or are they done?

00:43:23.731 --> 00:43:27.615
We can show user
some corner cases

00:43:27.616 --> 00:43:31.600
where they need to
provide more examples.

00:43:31.601 --> 00:43:33.960
But there are many
heuristics that are used

00:43:33.961 --> 00:43:36.865
inside this process that
can be machine-learnt.

00:43:36.866 --> 00:43:41.485
For instance, when to
stop asking questions?

00:43:41.486 --> 00:43:43.540
So every question
comes up with some kind

00:43:43.541 --> 00:43:45.515
of probability or threshold.

00:43:45.516 --> 00:43:46.880
And if it above the threshold,

00:43:46.881 --> 00:43:47.820
we will present it to the user.

00:43:47.821 --> 00:43:49.665
But what is that threshold?

00:43:49.666 --> 00:43:52.500
Or if we want to cluster

00:43:52.501 --> 00:43:55.645
the inputs based upon
their syntactic structure,

00:43:55.646 --> 00:43:57.430
this is again very machine
learning techniques

00:43:57.431 --> 00:44:00.550
can be quite useful.

00:44:00.551 --> 00:44:05.630
So, in order for us to make
big advances in this area,

00:44:05.631 --> 00:44:08.175
my partner in crime
has been Prateek Jain,

00:44:08.176 --> 00:44:13.265
who is a world-renowned machine
learning expert at MSRI.

00:44:13.266 --> 00:44:16.240
And he received great support

00:44:16.241 --> 00:44:19.420
from other colleagues
at the lab.

00:44:19.421 --> 00:44:21.060
And then he also
brought in a lot of

00:44:21.061 --> 00:44:23.680
resourcing in the form of
interns and research fellows.

00:44:23.681 --> 00:44:26.690
And this is what it takes
to start experimenting

00:44:26.691 --> 00:44:31.045
with such Cross-Disciplinary
research in a serious way.

00:44:31.046 --> 00:44:33.020
And again, all of
this arrangement was

00:44:33.021 --> 00:44:34.870
facilitated by Sriram Rajamani,

00:44:34.871 --> 00:44:36.155
who is the Lab Director,

00:44:36.156 --> 00:44:41.240
who wanted to bet on
this direction of research.

00:44:41.241 --> 00:44:45.770
So I'll conclude now.
So I talk to you about

00:44:45.771 --> 00:44:50.865
these Four Big Bets that have
brought in a lot of value.

00:44:50.866 --> 00:44:52.790
But there are also
new challenges that have come

00:44:52.791 --> 00:44:56.165
in and I'll just
summarize those here.

00:44:56.166 --> 00:44:59.590
So when we talk about
connecting with customers and

00:44:59.591 --> 00:45:03.170
gathering the real
data or maybe in

00:45:03.171 --> 00:45:04.650
the form of
telemetry or figuring

00:45:04.651 --> 00:45:06.230
out where my AI solution is

00:45:06.231 --> 00:45:10.350
not working well so that I
can improve my AI offering.

00:45:10.351 --> 00:45:11.980
We are going to have

00:45:11.981 --> 00:45:13.660
new challenges with
respect to collecting

00:45:13.661 --> 00:45:16.220
data because of
the GDPR requirements.

00:45:16.221 --> 00:45:18.210
So an interesting research
problem that this opens up

00:45:18.211 --> 00:45:21.320
is how can you get data back

00:45:21.321 --> 00:45:22.690
without violating any of

00:45:22.691 --> 00:45:25.690
the privacy issues that

00:45:25.691 --> 00:45:27.080
can yield you enough information

00:45:27.081 --> 00:45:29.625
that you can innovate
on your service.

00:45:29.626 --> 00:45:33.350
And we already actually have
a lot of real customer data

00:45:33.351 --> 00:45:36.190
and we want to release it in

00:45:36.191 --> 00:45:38.680
service of the academic
community and for

00:45:38.681 --> 00:45:42.090
other competitors to
try out newer ideas.

00:45:42.091 --> 00:45:43.940
But the problem is,
the damn problem is,

00:45:43.941 --> 00:45:47.120
that the data is so real that
we just cannot release it.

00:45:47.121 --> 00:45:48.990
It is the opposite problem now.

00:45:48.991 --> 00:45:50.990
So, we are not trying
to see if we can fake up

00:45:50.991 --> 00:45:53.180
this data so that
the real challenges of

00:45:53.181 --> 00:45:55.380
synthesis are still
preserved where the data is

00:45:55.381 --> 00:45:58.820
completely faked up and then
we can release this data.

00:45:58.821 --> 00:46:02.080
The other thing that I
talked about was taking

00:46:02.081 --> 00:46:05.390
a framework-based approach
to designing and development.

00:46:05.391 --> 00:46:08.495
And, one challenge that of
course always remains is,

00:46:08.496 --> 00:46:13.010
how to balance between
specialization and generalization.

00:46:13.011 --> 00:46:16.390
Because with a fixed amount

00:46:16.391 --> 00:46:19.100
of funding, no resources, time,

00:46:19.101 --> 00:46:21.250
either you can structure
something that is

00:46:21.251 --> 00:46:23.180
very intelligent for
a vertical domain or

00:46:23.181 --> 00:46:24.860
you can do something
that is less intelligent

00:46:24.861 --> 00:46:26.965
for a more in
a general purpose layer.

00:46:26.966 --> 00:46:28.380
So we always have to make

00:46:28.381 --> 00:46:32.565
the right set of choices
there, it is not easy.

00:46:32.566 --> 00:46:34.770
I third bet I talked about was

00:46:34.771 --> 00:46:37.520
putting research and
engineering together.

00:46:37.521 --> 00:46:40.180
And here, it even involves doing

00:46:40.181 --> 00:46:43.590
end-to-end delivery
of your offering.

00:46:43.591 --> 00:46:45.915
What about maintenance?

00:46:45.916 --> 00:46:48.180
Now, you put this thing
out and then are you

00:46:48.181 --> 00:46:51.215
committing to maintaining
it indefinitely?

00:46:51.216 --> 00:46:55.605
And even people management
isn't interesting

00:46:55.606 --> 00:46:57.490
unspoken aspect
here when you are

00:46:57.491 --> 00:47:01.635
running a big team of
people working together.

00:47:01.636 --> 00:47:04.550
And then the final thing
that I talked about was

00:47:04.551 --> 00:47:08.085
the significance of
Cross-Disciplinary Research.

00:47:08.086 --> 00:47:10.675
And one challenge
that comes here is,

00:47:10.676 --> 00:47:14.340
how do you balance between
doing some quick experiments

00:47:14.341 --> 00:47:16.430
versus doing things
in a way so that

00:47:16.431 --> 00:47:19.710
you are already, always
production ready.

